#!/usr/bin/env bash
cat <<'EOF'
OpenMANIPULATOR X grasp lab:

Terminal 1:
  omx-grasp-gz
  # random reachable object pose:
  omx-random-grasp-gz
  # repeated random grasp trials, auto reset after 3s hold:
  omx-random-grasp-loop
  omx-random-grasp-loop 5
  # three fixed benchmark patterns: near/front, middle/left-back, far/right-back.
  # Default is the fastest pipelined profile that has passed these three patterns:
  omx-three-pattern-grasp
  omx-three-pattern-grasp --profile safe

Terminal 2:
  omx-moveit-sim
  # or use GUI controls:
  omx-gui

Manual gripper / rough pose commands after Gazebo is running:
  omx-random-grasp
  omx-random-grasp --profile safe
  omx-random-grasp --profile turbo
  omx-random-grasp --profile max
  # comparison mode: run the same path without overlapping arm/gripper actions
  omx-random-grasp --profile max --sequential
  # calibrate the fast torque/gap gate against post-lift Gazebo truth labels
  omx-calibrate-grasp-gate 10
  omx-auto-grasp
  omx-open
  omx-pregrasp
  omx-lower
  omx-close
  omx-lift
  omx-home

Start with omx-random-grasp-gz, wait for Gazebo to finish loading, then run
omx-random-grasp in another terminal. The script uses analytic IK and a staged
above-descend-push-hold-lift path. In max mode, gripper opening overlaps the
first arm move, but the end effector stops at the grasp pose before closing.
The script reads the gripper action result and /joint_states effort/position;
it only lifts after contact is detected. Current fast gate is joint effort >=
180 and gripper gap >= 0.006 m, calibrated against random reachable poses.

For hands-off repeated trials, run omx-random-grasp-loop. Press Ctrl+C to stop.
EOF
